[SYNTAX]

Based on the appearance of tokens they are treated as different kinds of lists.

A list can contain a list inside itself.

The different control operators have a different level of relevance:

	Most relevant for lists creation:

	[BRACES]

	'( )'	Anything between braces is treated as a list on this list is only
			closed once the closing brace is encountered. Braces can only be
		placed on their own in a line (to execute commands in a subshell) or
		after a different operator liek AND, OR or pipes.

	[LOGICAL OPERATORS]
	
	'&&' and '||'	Anything in between any of these is treated and executed as
					part of a their list until another one of the logical
		operators or a pipe is encountered.

	[PIPE]

	'|'	Anything between to pipe symbols is treated as a list until another pipe
		or logical operator is encountered.

	[EXAMPLE]

	abronner@abronner-HP-Pavilion-g6-Notebook-PC:~$ echo HELLO && echo WORLD | cat -e && (echo WORLD) || echo END
		HELLO
		WORLD$
		WORLD

	Based on the output we can see that the line get's split into multiple
	lists:

	The lists in this are seperated by double qoutes

		"echo HELLO" && "echo WORLD" | "cat -e" "( "echo WORLD" )" || "echo END"

Lists associated with a logical operator don't get executed if their conditions
aren't met. If their conditions aren't met the previous return value is used to
evaluate the next logical operator:

	[EXAMPLE]

	abronner@abronner-HP-Pavilion-g6-Notebook-PC:~$ echo HELLO || echo WORLD && echo '!'
		HELLO
		!
	
	echo HELLO executes and has a exit value of 0 (success) so the next list
	after the OR operator doesn't get exectued. The 0 return value is then
	carried over to the next list. Since that list is also associated with a
	logical operator it is evaluated with the return value. Since the logical
	operator is a AND the next list get's executed.

This gives us a solid base for the structuring of a tree to chain the commands.

	The tree will execute in order.

	Every list in the tree is associated with either a logical operator, a pipe
	or a subshell.

[GROUP STRUCTS]

typedef struct s_group
{
	int		type;
	void	*data;
	int		logic;
	t_group	*next;
}	t_group;

typedef struct s_token
{
	size_t	size;
	int		*type;
	char	**content;
}	t_token;

The single commands in a line are seperated by control operators. These
operators either give them a context for exection or a context for grouping.

The commands can then be chained together in a proper tree by fitting them
together in a flexible list struct. The struct holds a pointer to either a new
list struct or a command token struct. They are differentiated by the data_type.

We can also split the control operators into grouping and exection operators.
Braces are grouping operators, logOps and pipes are exection operators. Each
command except the last one in a group needs an exection operator to tell
under which conditions the next command is supposed to be executed. 

Commands are chained together as groups. We differentiate betweeen two group
kinds: shell (or subshell) and pipes. 

	(Sub-)Shell groups split their tokens by logical operators.

	Pipes split their groups by the pipe symbol.

	Inside of either of those groups the other group can exists:

	[EXAMPLE]

	echo start && ( echo sub | echo shell && ( echo sub || echo sub | echo shell ) | echo pipe ) | echo pipe

	' 01 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- '
				  | 02 ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
				  ( 03 ----------------------------------------------------------------------- )
				    | 04 -------------- |    | 05 ------------------------------------------ |
				 							 ( 06 ------------------------------ )
														   | 07 -------------- |

	G ID	GROUP TYPE	GROUP TOKENS

	01		shell		'echo start' 'group 02'

	02		pipe		'group 03' 'echo pipe'

	03		subshell	'group 04' 'group 05'

	04		pipe		'echo sub' 'echo shell'

	05		pipe		'group 06' 'echo pipe'

	06		subshell	'echo sub' 'group 07'

	07		pipe		'echo sub' 'echo shell'

[EXECUTION]

For exection the lists will be traveresed, whenever a logOP is encountered the
process waits for a return value of the child process. Else it will continue
to create the apropriate processes. If a pipe is encoutnered it sets up the pipe
and proceeds after starting the child process.

If a new group (list) is encoutnered a chidl process is started to handle that
group and it's return value is used to evaluate potential logOPs. This process
continues until a LAST element in encountered. At which point the child process
stops execution. 

Redirection can only properly happen in the child process. Need for a function
that takes as input the command to be executed, the environment and the in, out
and err redirections.

An assignment can't happen for a whole pipe. It can only happen for single
commands or subshells.

[GENERAL COMMAND STRUCTURE]

A command consists of a cmdname, potential arguments, assignments and
redirections. Assignments always need to be infront of the cmdname, or rather
the first NON-Assignment in a split is the cmd. Everyhting after the command
that isn't a redirection is a argument to that command.

	[EXAMPLE]

	REDIRECTION ASSIGNEMENT ASSIGNMENT CMD ARG ARG ARG REDIRECTION ARG REDIRECTION
	i

	->	CUT OUT redirections

	ASSIGNEMENT ASSIGNMENT CMD ARG ARG ARG ARG
	i

	->	MAKE ASSIGNMENTS

	ASSIGNEMENT ASSIGNMENT CMD ARG ARG ARG ARG
						   i

[GENERAL SHELL STRUCTURE]

A subshell consists of brackets that group together some other commands or
groups to execute and potentially some redirections. A subshell doesn't take
anything except redirections in it's exection group.

	[EXAMPLE]

	redirection (cmd || cmd) redirection

		->	consists only of redirections in the execution group outside of the
			brackets.	->	Valid subshell

	assignment (cmd || cmd) redirection

		->	subshell after an assignment in the same execution group
			->	Invalid subshell ( '(' is the syntax error)

	redirection (cmd || cmd) assignment

		->	assignment after subshell in the same execution group
			->	Invalid assignment ( 'assignment' is the syntax error)

